WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.130
"The Suicide Squad" brings a whole bunch of
fascinating, little-known DC characters to

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.460
the silver screen.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:11.380
One of them is Peacemaker, a warrior who loves
peace so much, he'll kill to protect it.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.200
His story only gets weirder from there -- and
we're here to break it down for you.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.019
First things first: Who is Peacemaker?.

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:22.619
The central idea of Peacemaker is that he's
an incredibly devoted pacifist — so committed

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:26.590
to the cause, in fact, that he's willing to
hurt countless people to achieve it.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.349
That's right: He will wage war to win peace.

00:00:29.349 --> 00:00:30.789
"He loves peace.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:32.619
And he's willing to kill to get it.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.399
And that's pretty much it."

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:39.100
Though there are a few different incarnations
of the character, Christopher Smith, his original

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.170
incarnation, is the Peacemaker most fans know,
and the one relevant to "The Suicide Squad."

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:48.900
Smith was once a spy who worked to promote
the cause of world peace by sneaking into

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.950
various countries to fight warlords and dictators.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:57.480
He debuted in issue number 40 of "The Fightin'
5" way back in 1966, as the back-up story,

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.579
and then continued in his own comic ... which
lasted all of five issues.

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:07.270
Peacemaker was created by Charlton Comics'
heavy hitters Joe Gill and Pat Boyette.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:11.570
Though many of their other characters have
gone on to fame and fortune, Peacemaker hasn't

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.000
been quite so lucky.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.830
Though some undoubtedly loved him, he ended
up being a lesser-known superhero from a lesser-known

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:23.720
comic book publisher ... until DC Comics purchased
a huge swath of Charlton Comics characters

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.290
in the early 1980s.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:30.290
While it's incorrect to say that the DC purchase
brought Peacemaker out of obscurity, it did

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.950
save him from a totally trivial fate.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:38.320
Having purchased Charlton's characters, DC
began to incorporate them into their expansive

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.330
and frequently rebooted universe.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:45.770
While characters like Blue Beetle and Captain
Atom gained a new lease on life in DC's pages,

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:50.400
DC's attempt to revive Peacemaker wasn't terribly
successful on its own terms.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.770
DC's 1988 reboot took Peacemaker out of the
sparkling Silver Age of comics and into the

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.120
grit and grime of the late '80s.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.590
The short series pitches Peacemaker's absolute
commitment to peace as a mental illness.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:07.240
He suffers from extreme trauma and guilt,
as his father was a Nazi concentration camp

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.240
commander.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.920
To make matters worse, Peacemaker's father
committed suicide in front of him when he

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.750
was just a child, and the moment has stuck
with him ever since.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.920
Carrying his father's guilt and traumatized
by his death, Peacemaker is committed to stopping

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.840
the next Hitler by any means necessary.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.430
If that's not enough darkness for you, consider
this: This Peacemaker is haunted by the people

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:28.430
he's killed.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:34.000
Their ghosts, including his father's, speak
to him from inside his space-age bubble helmet.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.170
Combined with his new backstory, it's heavily
implied that the ghosts are nothing more than

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:42.890
a manifestation of his mental illness — but
this fact doesn't really lessen the grim shadow

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:44.250
they cast over this reboot.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:49.170
The 1988 "Peacemaker" reboot didn't make much
of a splash.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.780
Far and away the most likely reason comic
book readers today might have heard of the

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.750
Peacemaker, in fact, is a comic book that
doesn't even involve him.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.510
We're talking about "Watchmen," the legendary
comic series that is almost single-handedly

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:06.200
responsible for an entire generation of cynical,
bubble-popping takes on the superhero.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.690
Alan Moore and Dave Gibbons' inversion of
comic book myth-making didn't spring fully

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.620
formed from anyone's head, though.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.830
They used the DC-owned Charlton Comics characters
as their basis for Watchmen's super-powered

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.290
fascists, degenerates and madmen.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:24.650
Originally, the plan was to use the Charlton
characters directly, and Alan Moore's earliest

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:28.550
vision of the series revolved around the mystery
of Peacemaker's death.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:33.190
Uncomfortable with sending the Charlton characters
to such a dark fate, DC persuaded Moore into

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.650
swapping them out with original creations.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:40.950
Thus, the Watchmen cast was born, with their
Charlton heritage still very much intact.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.930
The Question morphed into the deranged Rorschach.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:47.250
Silk Spectre is a combination of Nightshade
and Phantom Lady.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.530
And Peacemaker became the Comedian, one of
the most well-known characters of the entire

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:52.530
series.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.760
Stripping away even the thinnest pretense
on Peacemaker's part, Moore built a terrible

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.599
attack dog who can be sicced on anyone threatening
American supremacy.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.830
Comic creators weren't content to let Peacemaker
become a footnote in future hardback editions

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:07.830
of "Watchmen."

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:12.140
The character reappeared in the late '80s,
joining the DC catch-all spy outfit Checkmate,

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.340
appearing in that book and "Suicide Squad"
as part of the late '80s "Janus Directive"

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:17.609
crossover event.

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:22.449
This storyline sees DC's various intelligence
organizations manipulated into fighting each

00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:23.449
other.

00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:27.129
It's a twist-filled storyline, and Peacemaker
is right in the middle of it.

00:04:27.129 --> 00:04:30.960
Yet again, however, his run in the comics
proved to be short-lived, even if he survived

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.719
that particular Suicide Squad adventure.

00:04:33.719 --> 00:04:37.341
That said, at least he goes out in dramatic
fashion this time, which is more than we can

00:04:37.341 --> 00:04:39.050
say for his earlier runs.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.860
In an attempt to stop the evil plans of the
villain Eclipso, Peacemaker crashes his helicopter

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:49.360
into the villain's tanks, seemingly dying
for good in a fiery explosion in 1993's "Eclipso"

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:50.360
#13.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:55.319
For a while, it looked like someone new would
take up the mantle of the late Peacemaker.

00:04:55.319 --> 00:04:59.469
A man named Mitchell Black dons a similar
costume and swipes the Peacemaker's motives

00:04:59.469 --> 00:05:02.699
in 1999's "Living Assault Weapons" #1.

00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:07.309
Rather than being haunted by lingering Nazi
guilt, Black is a disgraced former doctor

00:05:07.309 --> 00:05:10.430
who was stripped of his license after accidentally
killing a young patient.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:15.839
He soon meets the same fate as his bubble-headed
predecessor, however, dying during the 2005

00:05:15.839 --> 00:05:18.520
"Infinite Crisis" crossover event.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.560
Because comic book logic doesn't hold to our
real-world standards, it is later revealed

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.050
that Peacemaker number one, Christopher Smith,
is actually still alive.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:30.770
He enjoys quite a bit of spotlight in the
2000s "Blue Beetle" series, becoming a mentor

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:36.440
figure to young Jaime Reyes and a more self-actualized
person than he'd ever been before.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.039
Confusingly, there is yet another Peacemaker
of uncertain origin running around the modern

00:05:41.039 --> 00:05:42.389
era of comics.

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:48.349
This Peacemaker only shows up in 1994's "Justice
League International" #65 and "Justice League

00:05:48.349 --> 00:05:51.839
America" #90, and appears to be neither Smith
nor Black.

00:05:51.839 --> 00:05:53.249
So, who is he?

00:05:53.249 --> 00:05:57.800
Well, he's a member of the League-Busters,
who are apparently backed by the UN, and that's

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:58.810
all we know.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.809
He's never shown up since.

00:06:01.809 --> 00:06:04.770
"The Multiversity" was yet another chance
for comic book legend Grant Morrison to get

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:06.479
weird as all get-out.

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:11.860
The 2014 series sees a threat from outside
of DC's multiverse put the entirety of the

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:14.139
DC canon in danger of destruction.

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:19.119
In a series of one-offs and two collaborative
stories, C-list superheroes from all over

00:06:19.119 --> 00:06:24.809
the DC universe attempt to band together across
parallel timelines to stop these outsiders.

00:06:24.809 --> 00:06:28.659
These different planes of reality communicate
with one another through comic books, as the

00:06:28.659 --> 00:06:32.550
real superheroes of one timeline are fictional
in others.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:37.580
Peacemaker turns up in the "The Multiversity:
Pax Americana" #1, set on Earth-4, an Earth

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.419
inhabited by the creations of Charlton Comics.

00:06:40.419 --> 00:06:44.839
In "Pax Americana," Peacemaker follows his
commitment all the way to assassinating the

00:06:44.839 --> 00:06:46.520
President of the United States.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.240
It's fair to speculate that John Cena will
take Peacemaker in a different direction when

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.740
he dons the bubble helmet for "The Suicide
Squad."

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:57.039
In a featurette for the film, Cena describes
the character as:

00:06:57.039 --> 00:06:59.229
"A douchey Captain America."

00:06:59.229 --> 00:07:03.809
Given that punchy summation, Cena's comedic
acting chops, and the outright ridiculousness

00:07:03.809 --> 00:07:08.699
of Peacemaker's core character design, it
seems likely that the movie will use his deranged

00:07:08.699 --> 00:07:11.529
take on peacekeeping for more than a few laughs.

00:07:11.529 --> 00:07:16.110
From a bird's eye view of the DCEU as a whole,
we can't say we blame them — "Suicide Squad,"

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:20.300
with its gritty aesthetic and self-righteous
baddies, ended up being controversial, to

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:21.430
say the least.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:24.860
Might as well get a little goofy with the
dude who kills for peace, right?

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:28.860
Expect Peacemaker to be the butt of several
jokes, and a far cry from the deranged and

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:33.090
guilt-ridden Nazi descendant who chatted with
the ghosts of everyone he'd ever killed.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:37.450
While that Peacemaker was trying to make genuine
peace in the world and with his demons, it

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:42.289
seems like Cena's Peacemaker will be using
the olive branch mentality as a fig leaf for

00:07:42.289 --> 00:07:43.530
heinous actions.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:46.690
Oh, and you should also expect to see more
of him beyond the film.

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:51.819
Cena will get to live on in some form thanks
to a new HBO Max spinoff series, written by

00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:54.869
the film's director and screenwriter, James
Gunn.

00:07:54.869 --> 00:07:58.689
Gunn was pretty much given free rein to do
whatever he wanted with the Squad characters

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:01.509
for the film, and it's pretty clear DC likes
what they've seen.

00:08:02.509 --> 00:08:07.539
As part of DC Comics' line-wide event "Infinite
Frontier," in 2021 "Suicide Squad" was relaunched

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:12.699
with Christopher Smith — the original Peacemaker
— fronting a new version of Task Force X.

00:08:12.699 --> 00:08:16.099
Amanda Waller is more ambitious than ever
in the new series.

00:08:16.099 --> 00:08:21.369
In "Suicide Squad" #5, we learn Waller's using
Bloodsport to kidnap metahumans from different

00:08:21.369 --> 00:08:23.710
dimensions, bolstering her ranks.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:27.460
Peacemaker's job in Waller's plans is to help
her build a team meant to rival the Justice

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:31.710
League — and he not only seems pleased to
help, but he's just as coldblooded as his

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:32.829
new benefactor.

00:08:32.829 --> 00:08:37.000
By the end of "Suicide Squad" #2, five of
the team members under Peacemaker's command

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:41.610
are dead, and he doesn't seem to care about
it any more than if they were insects he didn't

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:42.909
notice he'd stepped on.

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:47.130
He even gets trapped in Arkham Asylum during
a deadly gas attack, and makes no attempt

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:51.199
at all to save any of the prisoners, security
guards, or other staff.

00:08:51.199 --> 00:08:53.110
All he cares about is the mission.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:57.410
So far, the new comic book series has shown
Peacemaker to be a fairly rare member of the

00:08:57.410 --> 00:08:58.410
Suicide Squad.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:02.959
Most Task Force X recruits would do anything
to get out from under Waller's boot, but not

00:09:02.959 --> 00:09:04.170
Christopher Smith.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:09.360
In his narration to "Suicide Squad" #2, Peacemaker
says he's grateful because he and Waller share,

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.970
quote, "the same mission."

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.120
Peace at any cost.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.060
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:18.199
Plus, even more Looper videos about DC Comics
are coming soon.

00:09:18.199 --> 00:09:20.779
